Maria (Support): Hey all — customer reporting intermittent JSON parse failures on their Go service when calling our streaming structured output. They see encoding/json errors like: "invalid character '\\n' in string literal". Attaching convo and quick repro they ran.

Alex H (Customer): FYI pasted their minimal repro below. Happens only when we stream (chunked) and when the output contains escaped unicode or backslashes next to newlines.

Alex H (Customer): ```bash
# curl streaming endpoint, structured mode
curl -N -H "Accept: application/json; mode=structured" \"https://api.redwood.ai/v1/generate?stream=true\" \ \ 
# example response chunks seen by app (not pretty printed)
{"id":"1","text":"Path: C:\\\\\Users\\\\Alex\\\\Documents\\n"}
{"id":"1","text":"More text; emoji: \\uD83D"}
```

Maria (Support): Their Go client errors when a chunk boundary splits an escape, e.g. backslash at end of chunk and the rest of the escape in next chunk (they suspect a surrogate pair like \\uD83D being split). They dug into encoding/json which rejects incomplete escapes.

Tim (Eng): Can reproduce locally with a small server that writes two chunks: first ends with a single backslash or a truncated \\uXXXX, second finishes the escape. Go decoder (streaming via Decoder) fails exactly as reported. Looks like our chunking passes raw bytes through without ensuring escape tokens are atomic.

Nora (SRE): Observed similar in prod traces — windows clients + high-latency regions produce more chunk splits. Likely the client buffer reads at different boundaries.

Tim (Eng): Quick triage notes:
- Root cause: chunk boundary can split a JSON string escape token (\\ or \\uXXXX or a UTF-16 surrogate) which leaves an invalid escape in the chunk passed to client decoder.
- Short-term workaround for customers: disable streaming (stream=false) or post-process on client to reassemble chunks before feeding to strict JSON parser. Another workaround: set response to ndjson with one JSON object per line (atomic lines) if suitable.

Maria (Support): Customer asked for repro steps to share with their infra team. Tim, can you add a minimal curl+node server repro we can send?

Tim (Eng): Pasting minimal Node repro (writes two chunks):
```js
const http = require('http');
http.createServer((req, res) => {
  res.writeHead(200, {'Content-Type':'application/json'});
  // first chunk ends with backslash then newline
  res.write('{"text":"hello \\');
  setTimeout(() => {
    res.write(' world"}');
    res.end();
  }, 20);
}).listen(8080);
```

Tim (Eng): If you run curl -N localhost:8080 you'll see two chunks. The Go stream decoder chokes if you feed it chunk-by-chunk.

Maria (Support): Customer also asked whether we can change server-side to avoid splitting escapes. Feasibility?

Tim (Eng): Short answer: doable but non-trivial. We can change our streaming flush boundaries so we don't flush mid-escape. That requires the runtime to be aware of JSON encoding state when emitting chunks (track if last byte is a backslash or inside \\u). Risk: small perf hit and more complex runtime logic.

Nora (SRE): Suggestion: implement a conservative fix first — if a chunk ends with a backslash or a partial \\u sequence, hold back up to X bytes and append to next chunk. That avoids most cases without expensive parsing.

Maria (Support): Customer wants ETA and whether billing is impacted (they're on Dedicated). Also asked if we can recommend quick client workaround.

Tim (Eng): ETA: I can draft a fix and run unit tests in ~3 days, roll to canary in 1 week if tests pass. Workaround for now: (1) set stream=false; (2) use a buffered reader on client that concatenates chunks until a full JSON object is available; (3) request NDJSON mode if acceptable.

Alex H (Customer): We're going to try client buffering for now. Thanks for the quick triage.

DeployBot: Deploy queued: streaming-chunk-atomic-fix (draft) by @tim in staging — run e2e when ready :rocket:

Maria (Support): Thanks team — sending repro + recommended workarounds to customer now. Will follow up if they still see failures after buffering on their side. :thumbsup:
